  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1139 PM CDT FRI SEP 11 2026\n\nEvening radar trends show convection gradually diminishing across\nmost of the area. However, a lingering mesoscale convective\nvortex (MCV) continues to spin across southwestern portions of\nCentral Alabama. As this feature tracks eastward overnight, it\nwill interact with richer moisture further southeast. The\ncombination of forced ascent and isentropic lift will support\nscattered showers and isolated thunderstorms into Saturday\nmorning, primarily across eastern Alabama. Given low to moderate\nconfidence in coverage, rain chances remain capped at 40 to\n60 percent through mid-morning.\n\nScattered to broad coverage of showers and thunderstorms is\nexpected to redevelop Saturday afternoon from west to east.\nPrecipitable water (PWAT) values remain well above average,\nensuring efficient rainfall production with stronger cores. While\ngeneral widespread rainfall totals will range from 0.5 to\n1.0 inch, localized heavier amounts are likely where persistent\nconvective training occurs. Severe weather and widespread flash\nflooding are not anticipated, though localized urban/low-lying\nnuisance flooding cannot be ruled out. Short-term model guidance\nexhibits variance regarding exact boundary placement and peak\ntiming; thus, POPs range from 40% west to 70% east through 7 PM,\nwith lingering shower activity persisting through 10 PM in the\nsoutheast.\n\nUpper-level ridging will begin building back into the deep South\non Sunday, leading to a marked decrease in precipitation\ncoverage. Convective activity will transition back to isolated,\ndiurnally driven afternoon showers and thunderstorms (<30%\nchance). Afternoon highs will rebound into the low-to-mid 90s by\nMonday. Broad ridging centered near the Tennessee Valley will\nmaintain this persistent warm and mostly dry pattern through the\nremainder of the forecast period.\n\n16",
